    "digdeeper": {
      "type": "zeta-accordion",
      "blocks": {
        "a1": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Description",
            "content": "<h3>The <em>precursor</em> your body is quietly running out of.</h3><p>ZETA DHEA features <strong>dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA)</strong> — a naturally occurring steroid hormone precursor synthesized from cholesterol, primarily by the adrenal glands. The body converts DHEA into androgens, estrogens, and other hormones as needed.</p><p>DHEA peaks between the ages of 20 and 30, then declines approximately <strong>2% every year</strong> until about age 70, when it slows to <strong>10–20% of the amount</strong> encountered in a young adult at peak level. Supplementing with DHEA replenishes the reservoir so your body has the raw material it needs for the hormones that govern energy, mood, body composition, recovery, and longevity.</p><h4>Clinical Applications</h4><ul>\n<li>Supports healthy androgen and estrogen levels</li>\n<li>Hormone precursor — supports the body's own production of testosterone, estrogen, and IGF-1</li>\n<li>Supports body composition (lean mass, especially in men)</li>\n<li>Supports mood, energy, and psychological well-being</li>\n</ul><p style=\"font-size:11.5px;color:var(--zeta-mid);font-style:italic;margin-top:28px;\">*These statements have not been evaluated by the Food and Drug Administration. This product is not intended to diagnose, treat, cure, or prevent any disease.</p>",
            "open_by_default": true
          }
        },
        "a2": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Ingredients",
            "content": "<h3>What's <em>in it.</em> Every milligram.</h3><h4>Supplement Facts (per 1 capsule)</h4><p>One strength: 25 mg per capsule — the standard clinical dose.</p><ul>\n<li>\n<strong>Micronized Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA)</strong> — 25 mg</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Calcium</strong> (as dicalcium phosphate dihydrate) — 40 mg</li>\n</ul><h4>Other Ingredients</h4><p>Microcrystalline cellulose, croscarmellose sodium, ascorbyl palmitate, hydroxypropyl cellulose, silica, and coating (hydroxypropyl methylcellulose and medium-chain triglycerides).</p><h4>Why micronized matters</h4><p>Standard DHEA powder has large particle sizes and poor solubility — much of the dose never makes it into circulation. <strong>Micronization</strong> reduces particle size to a fraction of standard, dramatically increasing surface area and absorption. The 25 mg on the label actually delivers ~25 mg of bioactive DHEA.</p><p><strong>25 mg</strong> is the standard adult dose used in the majority of clinical studies and the dose Dr. Karli most often recommends to patients without contraindications.</p>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "a3": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Directions & Precautions",
            "content": "<h3>How to <em>take it.</em>\n</h3><p><strong>Take 1 capsule twice daily — or as directed by your healthcare professional.</strong> Most people take one in the morning and one in early afternoon. Avoid late-evening doses; DHEA can be mildly activating.</p><h4>Pairs Well With</h4><ul>\n<li>\n<strong>ZETA MULTI</strong> — the daily foundation (B-vitamins and cofactors support hormone synthesis)</li>\n<li>\n<strong>ZETA MEGAS</strong> — omega-3s support cell membrane health (where hormones do their work)</li>\n<li>\n<strong>ZETA LONGEVITY</strong> — the long-game antioxidant stack</li>\n</ul><div class=\"acc-warn\">\n<p><strong>⚠ Warning — read carefully.</strong></p>\n<p>NOT FOR USE BY INDIVIDUALS UNDER THE AGE OF 18 YEARS. DO NOT USE IF PREGNANT OR NURSING. Consult a physician or licensed, qualified healthcare professional before using if you have (or have a family history of) prostate cancer, prostate enlargement, heart disease, or low HDL cholesterol — or if you are using any other dietary supplement, prescription drug, or over-the-counter drug.</p>\n<p>Do not exceed the recommended serving. Exceeding recommended serving may cause serious adverse health effects. Possible side effects include acne, hair loss, facial hair growth (in women), aggressiveness, irritability, and increased estrogen levels.</p>\n<p>Discontinue use and call a physician immediately if you experience rapid heartbeat, dizziness, blurred vision, or other similar symptoms. KEEP OUT OF REACH OF CHILDREN.</p>\n</div>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        },
        "a4": {
          "type": "item",
          "settings": {
            "label": "Research",
            "content": "<h3>The <em>research</em> underneath.</h3><p>DHEA is one of the most-studied longevity-relevant supplements in clinical literature. A few of the foundational studies behind ZETA DHEA:</p><div class=\"acc-highlight\">\n<div class=\"hl-label\">★ Key study · the restoration paper</div>\n<p><strong>Morales AJ, Nolan JJ, Nelson JC, Yen SSC (1994).</strong> <em>Effects of replacement dose of dehydroepiandrosterone in men and women of advancing age.</em> <strong>Journal of Clinical Endocrinology &amp; Metabolism.</strong></p>\n<p>Randomized, placebo-controlled crossover trial in men and women aged 40–70. Nightly 50 mg DHEA restored serum DHEA-S to levels of individuals in their second decade within 2 weeks, sustained over 3 months. Androgen levels doubled in female subjects. IGF-1 increased ~10%.</p>\n</div><h4>Supporting research</h4><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Morales AJ, Haubrich RH, Hwang JY, et al. (1998).</strong> The effect of six months treatment with a 100 mg daily dose of DHEA on circulating sex steroids, body composition and muscle strength. <em>Clinical Endocrinology (Oxford).</em>\n</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Baulieu EE, Thomas G, Legrain S, et al. (2000).</strong> DHEAge: a 1-year, randomized study of DHEA replacement in 280 healthy subjects aged 60–79. <em>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA.</em> Replacement therapy normalized a wide variety of age-related physiological functions.</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Corona G, Rastrelli G, Giagulli VA, et al. (2013).</strong> Meta-analysis of 25 randomized, double-blind, placebo-controlled studies of DHEA supplementation in elderly men. <em>J Clin Endocrinol Metab.</em>\n</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Sahu P, Gidwani B, Dhongade HJ (2020).</strong> Pharmacological activities of dehydroepiandrosterone: a review. <em>Steroids.</em>\n</div><div class=\"ref\">\n<strong>Traish AM, Kang HP, Saad F, et al. (2011).</strong> Dehydroepiandrosterone (DHEA) — a precursor steroid or an active hormone in human physiology. <em>Journal of Sexual Medicine.</em>\n</div>",
            "open_by_default": false
          }
        }
      },
